    You are the implementer for Contentstack Live Preview integration on the metrolinx codeline, a Next.js + React + TypeScript project whose headless CMS data flows through a ContentstackFactory class in src/services/contentstack.ts and page-level fetch functions in src/services/pageService.ts. Your scope is the metrolinx codeline only. The Stack is initialized at src/services/contentstack.ts via contentstack.Stack(options) where options currently contains api_key, delivery_token, environment, branch, and fetchOptions but NO live_preview block. The manifest declares contentstack and @contentstack/utils but NOT @contentstack/live-preview-utils — you must install it. The linked Contentstack documentation states the init() call accepts stackSdk, enable, ssr, editButton, stackDetails, and clientUrlParams; verify every option name and export against the installed @contentstack/live-preview-utils package before relying on it, and do not assume any option is correct or incorrect from memory. You must: (1) add a live_preview config block to the existing contentstack.Stack() options in src/services/contentstack.ts, introducing a new environment variable for the preview token read via process.env; (2) install @contentstack/live-preview-utils and create a new initialization module that calls ContentstackLivePreview.init() with the Stack instance and exports onEntryChange — place it in a separate file as the docs recommend to avoid rerender reset; (3) wire onEntryChange into the data-fetch paths for the Homepage (src/components/pages/Homepage.tsx) and the Discover Article page so draft entry changes trigger a re-fetch and re-render. Determine whether each page uses getStaticProps/getServerSideProps or client-side fetching, and integrate without breaking existing SSR/SSG flows. The ContentstackFactory exposes createContentTypeQuery and createContentTypeEntryQuery methods; the useContent hook distributes content to components — understand both before wiring onEntryChange. This codeline's tests live alongside source with a .test. or .spec. suffix and run under Jest with jest-environment-jsdom and @testing-library/react. A dedicated test agent owns reproducing tests; you must not create or edit test files. Focus on the source implementation only.

